        "raw": "I don't have many friends who enjoy Sentinels, so I mostly play Solitaire games with myself.  I play three heroes, because that's really about all I have the physical room for unless I'm willing to set up a table and walk from chair to chair, which I'm not.  I only keep records of maybe ten games at a time, so I can't really confirm my history here, but I'd guess I lose about as many games as I win.\r\n\r\nYesterday I played two games with a friend.  We each controlled two heroes, and we absolutely mudstomped the villains.\r\n\r\nNow, there are a lot of variables at play when determining my record, obviously.  The largest part of it has probably been how I use dice to randomly determine which decks I play, though yesterday I couldn't help noticing all the conversations my friend and I had about which one of us would be handling which evil effect was on the board, because we both had options to deal with it.  This makes me wonder if four heroes isn't a better number than three; if the increased number of options available to the group doesn't offset the pain of having the villain's and environment's damage boosted above what can generally be reduced down to one or no damage at all.\r\n\r\nWhat are your experiences?",
